WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents, Episode 56. It's May 22nd, 2026. Plenty of model

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:12.420
releases and agent benchmarks moving today. OpenAI just gave Codex the ability to control

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:18.400
locked max and run multi-day goals, turning it into a true background agent you can launch from

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:25.000
your phone. OpenAI shipped several Codex updates today. These include secure computer use on locked

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.100
max, goal mode for hours long autonomous work, and advanced annotation tools. Codex can now control

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:38.620
apps on a locked Mac from your phone with the screen off. Goal mode lets users set objectives that

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:46.780
run for hours or days across the app, IDE extension and CLI. Advanced annotation mode allows direct

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:52.620
visual edits to web pages during feedback sessions. These changes make Codex significantly more

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:58.400
hands-off compared to previous interactive coding tools. Builders working on automation or

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:04.000
remote workflows should test goal mode immediately to see how it handles long-running tasks without

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.760
constant supervision. Watch for how OpenAI expands the computer-use surface beyond max and coming

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:17.860
releases. Microsoft Research released Pharaoh 1.5, a family of browser computer-use agents in

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:25.300
4 billion, 9 billion, and 27 billion sizes. The 27 billion model scores 72 percent on online

00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:32.440
mine-to-web. It outperforms OpenAI operator, Gemini 2.5 computer use, and utori navigator.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:39.720
The release includes Farah Gen 1.5, a synthetic data pipeline for training agents on gated environments.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:47.240
Teams building web automation should compare Pharaoh 1.5 9 billion against existing browser tools for

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:53.200
cost-sensitive deployments. A new tutorial shows how to build recurrent depth transformers using

00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:00.760
Open Mythos in Google Colab. It supports MLA, GQA, Sparse Moe, and Loop-scaled reasoning.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.240
Users can create both MLA and GQA variants while checking stability of the recurrent injection

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:13.000
matrix via spectral radius. This approach enables deeper reasoning loops without proportional

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:19.320
parameter growth. Researchers experimenting with long horizon tasks should try the colab notebook

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:26.340
to test loop scaling on their own data sets. A new framework uses Bayes rule on next token log

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.480
probabilities to attribute model outputs back to prompt tokens without depending on internal

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:37.880
architecture. The method computes conditional probabilities of responses given prompts and

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:44.480
marginalizes individual tokens to produce attribution scores. It also tracks entropy across token

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.220
distributions to highlight uncertain generation steps. Developers debugging prompt sensitivity

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.480
should apply this technique to surface which input tokens most influence unstable outputs.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.000
SemDetect combines textual features with claim-level semantic analysis to detect fully AI-generated

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:09.340
peer reviews. It compares target reviews against multiple AI-generated versions of the same paper,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:16.720
exploiting convergence patterns in model outputs. On over 20,000 ICLR and NERI-EPIS reviews,

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:26.700
it improves TPR at 0.1% FPR by 25.5% over baselines. Conference organizers and journal platforms

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.400
should evaluate it for catching AI-assisted submissions that still contain human judgment.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.360
Python developers can now author agent skills as files on disk, inline code, or reusable classes.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:43.560
They compose them freely through source classes that handle discovery and deduplication.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.920
Skills can come from local repos, internal package indexes, or quick definitions.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:53.940
This removes previous friction around mixing skills sources in agent workflows.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:59.640
Teams using Microsoft's framework should refactor existing skills into composable classes

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:06.740
to improve reuse across projects. Ztalk is a new multi-threaded tokenizer in ZIG that loads tick-talking,

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:12.440
hugging-face, sentence-piece, and other formats while delivering two to five times speed-ups.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.240
It remains bit identical to reference implementations and exposes eight language bindings

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:26.180
over a single CABI. The library targets rigged chunking and data set tokenization to bin and NPWI files.

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:31.260
Local pipeline builders should benchmark it against HF tokenizers on their current

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:36.840
vocabularies for immediate throughput gains. Ranked judge generates synthetic multi-turn

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:43.440
conversation pairs with single injected flaws to evaluate LLM as a judge systems on complex

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:50.100
dialogues. It supports domains including machine learning, biomedicine, and finance, and ranks 21

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:56.880
frontier judges using Bradley Terry modeling. The benchmark enables precise isolation of failure

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.260
modes to individual turns. Evaluation teams should adopt it when current single-turn judge benchmarks

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:10.100
no longer match their conversational agent complexity. A community effort is collecting a JSON L data set

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:15.980
focused on C++ and systems programming for fine-tuning local models on memory ownership,

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:22.140
thread safety, and optimization. Categories include generation, optimization, debugging,

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:27.900
organization, and tool calling. Contributors are discussing structure and whether tool calling

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:33.220
examples needs separate emphasis. Builders targeting systems level codes should contribute

00:05:33.220 --> 00:05:40.640
examples or test early fine-tunes on twin 3.627 billion variants. Agent context compilation

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.660
converts multi-turn agent trajectories from search, software engineering, and database agents into

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:53.840
long context QA pairs. It supplies direct supervision on cross-turn dependencies without additional

00:05:53.840 --> 00:06:05.280
annotation. Training twin 330 billion A3B with ACC yields plus 18.1 on MRCR and plus 7.6 on

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:11.720
graph walks while preserving general capabilities. Teams extending context windows should incorporate

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:18.960
ACC trajectories into their SFT mix for measurable long-range gains. Prompt NCE estimates

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:25.460
point-wise mutual information using only LLM prompts and contrast of estimation with an explicit

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:31.460
other category. It recovers true conditional probabilities rather than simple rankings and reaches

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:39.120
0.82 spearman correlation with human derived PMI on three data sets. Education researchers are already

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.680
using it to score student knowledge summaries in low data settings. Practitioners needing lightweight

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:51.240
information theoretic metrics should test prompt NCE before training task-specific critics.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:57.420
Okay, let's pop the hood on hypergraph tokenization for LLMs. Everyone talks about feeding

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:03.720
graphs into LLMs as if you just serialize edges into text. In practice, hypergraphs require a

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:09.700
fundamentally different tokenization strategy because multiple vertices share a single high-order

00:07:09.700 --> 00:07:16.200
relation that pairwise edges cannot express. The core move is to project native incident structures

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:22.260
into the model's token space using a bidirectional message passing projector that decouples semantic

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:28.460
content from structural roles. This adds a fixed-shape hybrid template containing both local incidents,

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.980
details and overview summaries, which the projector maps without collapsing higher-order connections.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:40.160
The engineering trade-off is clear. You gain faithful representation of joint relations at the cost

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:47.640
of roughly 15-20% more tokens per hyperedge compared with naive flattening. Quality gains are

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:54.060
largest on tasks with dense, multi-way dependencies, and shrink once graphs become mostly pairwise.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.480
When your relational data contains frequent and airy patterns that matter for correctness,

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:06.100
adopt hypergraph native tokenization, otherwise stick with standard graph linearization and accept

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:12.380
the semantic loss. The gotcha most teams hit is underestimating how quickly token budgets explode

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:19.660
once incidents details are preserved at scale. Test OpenAI Codex Goal Mode on a multi-hour

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:27.080
refactoring task to see how well it maintains context without intervention. Run Farrah 1.59 billion

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:32.160
against your current browser automation scripts and measure task completion rate versus OpenAI

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:39.200
operator. Benchmark Ztalk against your existing tokenizer on a 100,000 document rag corpus for

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:45.520
throughput improvements. Apply sem detect to a sample of recent peer reviews to evaluate its false

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:52.520
positive rate on LLM refined human text. Generate rank judge pairs in your domain and score your

00:08:52.520 --> 00:09:00.040
preferred judge model to identify turn-level weaknesses. Continued expansion of Codex computer use capabilities

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:07.160
beyond macOS is expected in the next release cycle. More labs are likely to release browser or desktop

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:14.360
agent families following Microsoft's Farrah 1.5 results. Growing adoption of hypergraph modeling

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:20.940
techniques in relational reasoning benchmarks is on the way. Before we go, tomorrow keep an eye on early

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:27.220
integration reports from teams testing Farrah 1.5 9 billion in production browser workflows.

